    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "the intensity is super high, like a sharp pull in my chest. i dont have physio data but i can definitely log it. i've been tracking it for a few weeks and it's almost always right. like, if i feel a 0.8/0.9 spike and then nothing happens, i realize i misread the math, but when it hits, it's almost always a high-impact event. \n\nif we use a bayesian framework, can we treat my intuition as a prior that updates in real time? i want to see if we can actually calculate the signal-to-noise ratio of these spikes. how would we structure the likelihood function if my 'signal' is basically an instantaneous update to the probability of an event?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
